{
  "dn26:0.1": "Long Discourses 26 ",
  "dn26:0.2": "The Wheel-Turning Monarch ",
  "dn26:1.0": "1. Taking Refuge in Oneself ",
  "dn26:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "dn26:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Magadhans at Mātulā. ",
  "dn26:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "dn26:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "dn26:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "dn26:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "dn26:1.7": "“Mendicants, live as your own island, your own refuge, with no other refuge. Let the teaching be your island and your refuge, with no other refuge. ",
  "dn26:1.8": "And how does a mendicant do this? ",
  "dn26:1.9": "They meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. ",
  "dn26:1.10": "They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … ",
  "dn26:1.11": "mind … ",
  "dn26:1.12": "principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. ",
  "dn26:1.13": "That’s how a mendicant lives with themselves as island and refuge, without other refuge; with the teachings as island and refuge, without other refuge. ",
  "dn26:1.14": "You should roam inside your own territory, the domain of your fathers. ",
  "dn26:1.15": "If you roam inside your own territory, the domain of your fathers, Māra won’t find a vulnerability or get an opportunity. ",
  "dn26:1.16": "It is due to undertaking skillful qualities that merit grows. ",
  "dn26:2.0": "2. King Daḷha